# What the running API queries through. On a managed Postgres this is the POOLED
# endpoint — Neon's host with `-pooler` in it.
DATABASE_URL="postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/fotspot"

# What migrations connect through, and it is REQUIRED: Prisma refuses to load a
# schema whose env vars do not resolve, so an unset DIRECT_URL fails
# `prisma generate` at build time rather than at runtime.
#
# One Postgres means one endpoint, so set it to the same value as DATABASE_URL.
# On Neon it is the UN-POOLED host — the same URL without `-pooler`, which Neon
# hands you as DATABASE_URL_UNPOOLED. The pooler is PgBouncer in transaction mode,
# and `migrate deploy` takes an advisory lock that has to outlive one transaction
# to stop two deploys migrating at the same time.
DIRECT_URL="postgresql://postgres:postgres@localhost:5432/fotspot"

# Used by RedisService (README 1.19). The API degrades to Postgres-only reads if
# Redis is unreachable, so this is not required to boot.
#
# BullMQ uses the same server on its own connection (see app.module.ts). Uploads
# are finalised by a worker on that queue, so with Redis down clips stay
# PROCESSING until it returns — they are not lost, only not yet visible.
#
# Keep this pointing at the docker-compose Redis in development, and Postgres
# above likewise: a dev run that can reach production is one stray migration away
# from rewriting it.
#
# Upstash in production: use their **TCP** endpoint, `rediss://default:<token>@
# <name>.upstash.io:6379`, not the REST URL. BullMQ is built on blocking commands
# (BZPOPMIN) held open on a live socket, and `@upstash/redis` speaks HTTP
# request/response — it cannot block, so the queue would never receive a job. The
# TCP endpoint is ordinary Redis and needs no code change at all.
#
# Then turn eviction OFF (Upstash → Database → Configuration → Eviction). BullMQ
# warns `eviction policy is optimistic-volatile, it should be noeviction` at boot,
# and it is right: a queue is not a cache, and an evicted job is work nobody knows
# was lost. It cannot be set over the wire — Upstash refuses CONFIG SET.
REDIS_URL="redis://localhost:6379"

# The API refuses to start in production while either of these is missing, still
# the example value, under 32 characters, or equal to the other — see
# src/common/security.config.ts. Generate each with:
#   openssl rand -base64 48
JWT_ACCESS_SECRET="change-me-access"
JWT_ACCESS_TTL="15m"
JWT_REFRESH_SECRET="change-me-refresh"
JWT_REFRESH_TTL="30d"

# Browser origins allowed to call this API, comma-separated. Empty in
# development reflects whatever asked (so `next dev` on a shifting port works);
# empty in production allows no browser origin at all, because a missing security
# setting must fail closed rather than revert to allow-all.
CORS_ORIGINS=""

# How many proxies actually sit in front of this process — 1 behind a single load
# balancer, 2 behind a CDN and a balancer.
#
# It decides which entry of `X-Forwarded-For` is treated as the real client, and
# both the login lockout and the request throttle key on that address. Leave at 0
# unless something in front genuinely appends the header: trusting it when nothing
# does lets any caller mint a fresh identity per request and walk through both.
TRUST_PROXY_HOPS="0"

# Google sign-in. The OAuth 2.0 **Web application** client id from
# console.cloud.google.com → APIs & Services → Credentials. The client *secret* is
# not needed and must not be put here: the browser gets an ID token from Google
# and this server only verifies its signature against Google's public keys.
#
# Add the app's origins to "Authorised JavaScript origins" on that same client,
# or Google refuses to render the button. The client needs the same value as
# NEXT_PUBLIC_GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID in client/.env.
GOOGLE_CLIENT_ID=""

# Telegram sign-in. The bot token from @BotFather — this is a SECRET, and it is
# the key the Login Widget's signature is checked with, so anybody holding it can
# mint a valid sign-in for any Telegram account.
#
# Also send @BotFather `/setdomain` for the site the button is served from, or
# the widget refuses to load. The client needs the bot's *username* as
# NEXT_PUBLIC_TELEGRAM_BOT_USERNAME; only this server sees the token.
TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN=""

# Transactional email — Resend (https://resend.com).
#
# Sends the registration code, the password-reset code and the contact-change
# code. Without it those are still generated and stored, and in development still
# echoed back as `devCode`, so local work needs no key.
#
# The sender must be on a domain **verified in Resend**. A subdomain is the right
# choice: `email.fotspot.uz` carries its own SPF and DKIM records, so a burst of
# transactional mail cannot damage the reputation of the address people reply to.
# Verify the domain in Resend first, add the DNS records it gives you, and wait
# for it to show as verified — an unverified sender is refused outright.
RESEND_API_KEY=""
EMAIL_FROM="FotSpot <noreply@email.fotspot.uz>"

OTP_TTL_SECONDS="300"

# Cloudflare R2 (S3-compatible) - plug real values in production
#
# One API token must be authorized for BOTH buckets below. A token scoped to one
# of them fails only on the uploads that touch the other, which reads as "avatars
# are broken" rather than as a permissions problem. `pnpm r2:check` says so
# plainly.
R2_ACCOUNT_ID=""
R2_ACCESS_KEY_ID=""
R2_SECRET_ACCESS_KEY=""

# The PRIVATE bucket: player clips and their cover frames, plus the §12.1
# identity documents. Nothing here is reachable without a signature the API mints
# per read. It must have no public access of any kind — a clip is a video of a
# child, and a public address for one is an address nobody can revoke.
R2_PRIVATE_BUCKET=""

# The PUBLIC bucket, served at R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL: avatars, academy logos and
# academy gallery images — what an account published as its own face.
#
# Keys route by their `public/` or `private/` prefix, so a key's tier decides
# which bucket it is written to and read from, and the two cannot disagree.
#
# Leave empty for a single-bucket deployment, where this falls back to R2_PRIVATE_BUCKET
# and both tiers share it. That works, but then the `public/` prefix is only a
# declaration: R2 public read MUST be scoped to it, or `private/players/…` is
# anonymously downloadable at the host below whatever the code intends.
R2_PUBLIC_BUCKET=""

# Origins allowed to upload straight to R2 (see `pnpm r2:cors`, which applies the
# policy to both buckets — the browser PUTs to each).
# The backend holds port 3000, so `next dev` serves the client on 3001.
R2_CORS_ORIGINS=""

# The CDN origin serving R2_PUBLIC_BUCKET. This and R2_PUBLIC_BUCKET must name
# the same bucket: pointing this at a bucket the app never writes to leaves every
# upload succeeding and every image 404ing, with nothing in the logs. Set both,
# then run `pnpm r2:check` — it writes a probe object and fetches it back.
#
# The client needs the same value as NEXT_PUBLIC_MEDIA_URL in client/.env.
R2_PUBLIC_BASE_URL=""

PORT=3000
